course: dictionary
lesson: 1307
title: Cacao: a tree, its beans and the chocolate they make
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cacao is a noun, a naming word. You can find its meaning from the words around it. «Chocolate comes from cacao beans, the seeds of a tree from South and Central America.» The sentence tells you that cacao is a tree, and its beans are the seeds that chocolate is made from.
explain: Now read «Cocoa pods grow straight out of the trunk of the cacao tree.» Here cacao means the tree itself. The clue words trunk and pods show what is growing. Check by swapping in the tree: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Cacao can also mean the beans, or the raw powder made from them. In «The Maya and Aztecs made a bitter, frothy chocolate drink from cacao beans», the beans are the ingredient. Look at the words around cacao each time to decide which meaning fits.
explain: On a food label, «cacao» usually means the product has been processed less than «cocoa». When context does not settle it, a dictionary will.
fact: Cacao is a noun. It can name a tree, its beans, or the powder made from them.
fact: «Cacao» means a tropical tree whose seeds are used to make chocolate and cocoa.
fact: «Cacao beans» are the seeds, and they grow in big pods on the trunk of the tree.
fact: The Aztecs drank a bitter chocolate drink made from cacao beans, and they even used the beans as money.
fact: An English firm sold one of the first chocolate bars in 1847.
exercise: «Cocoa pods grow straight out of the trunk of the cacao tree.» What is the cacao in this sentence? | A hot sweet drink | *A tree | A bag of sugar | A bar of chocolate
exercise: On a food label, «cacao» usually means the product is less processed than «cocoa». What does that tell you? | Cacao is always sweeter | Cacao is always made in a factory | *Cacao is usually less processed | Cacao is a kind of sugar
exercise: Where are cacao beans grown, according to the text? | At the North Pole | Only in English shops | Deep under the sea | *In West Africa, South and Central America
test: «Cacao beans were even used as money.» What does this tell you about cacao beans? | They were made of metal | *They were valuable to the Aztecs | They were only ever eaten as sweets | They were thrown away after use
test: How many beans can one cocoa pod hold, according to the text? | Over 1,000 | None at all | Exactly 5 | *About 20 to 50
exam: Which sentence uses cacao to mean the beans or powder, not the tree? | «The cacao tree grows near the equator.» | «Cocoa pods grow straight out of the trunk of the cacao tree.» | *«The Aztecs drank a bitter chocolate drink made from cacao beans.» | «The cacao tree stood tall in the forest.»
